# Keep the dependency lockfiles LF on every platform so the CI sync-check (`uv export` +
# `git diff --exit-code`) can't be tripped by Windows CRLF drift (DEP-1).
uv.lock text eol=lf
requirements.lock text eol=lf
docker/locks/*.lock text eol=lf
# constraints.lock was MISSING here (`git check-attr text -- constraints.lock` -> unspecified), so under
# core.autocrlf=true it checks out CRLF — precisely the drift this stanza exists to prevent. Harmless so
# far only because git's clean filter normalizes before `git diff`, which is also why export sync must be
# verified with `git diff` and never a raw `diff` (the latter reports a phantom whole-file difference).
constraints.lock text eol=lf
# The PEP 735 CI-toolchain exports (ADR 0034 §3) — same DEP-1 diff gate, same LF requirement.
ci/locks/*.lock text eol=lf
.mefor-hooks/** text eol=lf
# Shell scripts are executed on the Linux CI runners; force LF so a contributor's
# core.autocrlf=true can never commit CRLF (which breaks `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`).
*.sh text eol=lf

# The vendored CLA Action's compiled bundle (BACKLOG #1364): its SHA256 was checked against the
# exact upstream commit and that checksum is recorded as evidence in the ledger entry. `-text`
# disables ALL line-ending conversion so the stored and checked-out bytes never drift from what
# was verified -- `eol=lf` was tried first and silently re-encoded ~1,300 bytes, which would have
# committed a file that no longer matches its own recorded checksum.
.github/actions/**/dist/** -text

# Everything that ships must have platform-independent bytes (BACKLOG #1442). Without this,
# `core.autocrlf=true` converts on checkout, so a wheel built from a Windows tree carries a
# byte-different copy of every text file than the released Linux one and the two wheels'
# `*.dist-info/RECORD` rows disagree -- different sha256, different size -- while the content is
# identical. Measured 2026-09-03: nine shipped files drifted, 15,256 converted line endings in
# messagefoundry/auth/data/common_passwords.txt alone.
#
# THE COST IS A VERIFICATION GAP, not a live defect. release.yml builds on ubuntu-latest only, so
# every published artifact is the LF build, and the SLSA provenance binds that artifact's sha256 to
# this source commit. An operator who rebuilds from the commit to check that binding -- the point of
# the attestation -- reproduces the digest on Linux and cannot on Windows, with nothing explaining
# why. This does NOT affect the ADR 0041 D3 startup attestation, which sources its baseline from the
# installed distribution's own RECORD (messagefoundry/integrity.py, `dist.read_text("RECORD")`), so
# baseline and installed file come from one install and cannot be split by a line-ending policy.
#
# `text=auto`, NEVER a bare `text`, on any pattern that can match a binary. Measured: bare
# `text eol=lf` strips CRLF byte pairs from inside a binary blob (a 17-byte fixture stored as 14);
# `text=auto` defers to git's binary detection and stores it intact. The 19 tray/assets/*.ico files
# survive a bare `text` today only because none happens to contain a CRLF pair.
#
# `eol=lf` and NOT `-text`, which the vendored CLA bundle above uses for a reason that does not
# transfer. That bundle's blob genuinely holds CRLF (1,297 pairs -- it is one of only 4 such blobs in
# 2,061 tracked files) and its recorded SHA256 would break on re-encoding. Every file below already
# stores pure LF, so `eol=lf` re-encodes nothing and all three spellings yield the same bytes on
# disk. What separates them is what happens next: `-text` disables the clean filter and merely
# FREEZES, so a later Windows editor save commits CRLF permanently, while `eol=lf` normalizes on the
# way in. For files whose bytes anything downstream records, enforcing LF is what keeps that record
# true.
#
# The root patterns are ANCHORED with a leading slash. Unanchored, `README.md` also matches
# messagefoundry/generators/README.md, and the five root names pin 11 files outside the shipped set.
messagefoundry/** text=auto eol=lf
/LICENSE text=auto eol=lf
/NOTICE text=auto eol=lf
/README.md text=auto eol=lf
/CHANGELOG.md text=auto eol=lf
/pyproject.toml text=auto eol=lf
